{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghbfmQjXRxQPNUEqrhrC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Approach.** Tetrahedral d⁸ has a ³T₁(F) ground state. The three bands are ν₁ = ³T₂(F), ν₂ = ³A₂(F) and ν₃ = ³T₁(P), all measured from ³T₁(F). The energy matrix is the same as for octahedral d² with positive Dq, using the F–P coupled matrix.\n\n**Energies relative to the uncoupled ³T₁(F) zero:**\n- ³T₂(F) lies at +2Dq.\n- ³A₂(F) lies at +12Dq.\n- ³T₁(F) and ³T₁(P) mix through the matrix [[−6Dq, 4Dq], [4Dq, 15B]].\n- I fixed the off-diagonal term and the ³T₁(P) diagonal term by requiring the strong-field limit to give the t₂² and t₂e ³T₁ energies (−8Dq and +2Dq).\n\n**Solving:**\n1. ν₂ − ν₁ = 10Dq, because the ground-state shift cancels in the difference. So 10Dq = 7870 − 3600 = 4270 cm⁻¹, giving Dq ≈ 427 cm⁻¹.\n2. From ν₁, the ground-state energy is E_g = 2Dq − ν₁ = 854 − 3600 = −2746 cm⁻¹.\n3. E_g is the lower root of the ³T₁ matrix, so (−6Dq − E_g)(15B − E_g) = 16Dq². This gives 184 × (15B + 2746) ≈ 2.92 × 10⁶, so 15B ≈ 13 100 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 875 cm⁻¹.\n4. Check on ν₃: the upper root is about 13 290 cm⁻¹, so ν₃ ≈ 13 290 + 2746 ≈ 16 040 cm⁻¹. The observed value is 16 410 cm⁻¹, which is about 2% off and acceptable.\n\n**Result:** Dq ≈ 427 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 875 cm⁻¹. This is about 85% of the free-ion B of roughly 1040 cm⁻¹. C is not determined by these three spin-allowed bands, so I give no value for it.\n\n{\"Dq\": 427, \"B\": 875, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fitted ν1, ν2 and ν3 with the Td d8 ³T1(F)–³T1(P) matrix; ν3 is reproduced within about 2%, and C cannot be determined from spin-allowed bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1649,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1649,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"sta